<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Isaaq Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1750 CE – 1884 CE (approx.) · capital Toon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A sultanate of the Isaaq clans of northern Somalia whose sultans mediated clan affairs from Toon until the British protectorate.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>According to tradition the Isaaq clans chose a common sultan in the mid-18th century after a victory over rival clans at the battle of Lafaruug. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guled-abdi">Guled Abdi</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eidagale">Eidagale</a> became the first sultan, and his descendants, the Rer Guled, held the title, with their seat at Toon near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hargeisa">Hargeisa</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultan was a mediator and war leader rather than an absolute ruler, presiding over councils of elders who applied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> customary law, xeer, and Islamic law. The Isaaq economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> and sheep herding and on trade through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbera">Berbera</a>, whose annual fair drew merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Between 1884 and 1886 Isaaq clans signed treaties with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, and their lands became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-somaliland">British Somaliland</a>. Some Isaaq branches had their own sultans, such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/habr-yunis-sultanate">Habr Yunis Sultanate</a>. The Isaaq heartland is today the core of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-somaliland">Republic of Somaliland</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/isaaq-sultanate.jpg" alt="Isaaq Sultanate" width="320" height="203"><figcaption>Image: Al Sheikh Abdullah Ray is a Somali · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isaaq_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1750 CE – 1884 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Toon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Guled Abdi, Farah Guled, Deria Hassan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Somali</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/somalia">Somalia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Isaaq, Sultanate of Isaaq</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1817&amp;focus=isaaq-sultanate" aria-label="Isaaq Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5896 1820 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Isaaq Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6216 2076L6240 2044"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6240 2044h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6216 2076h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6216 2076h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1817&amp;focus=isaaq-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
